WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.802
-I want to start you're
a national security reporter,

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:05.505
I think oftentimes when we see
those of your profession

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:08.208
on television
shows or in films,

00:00:08.208 --> 00:00:10.910
it seems very fast paced,
very cinematic job.

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:12.244
Is that true in real life?

00:00:12.244 --> 00:00:16.015
-So usually when you're
in the movies watching

00:00:16.015 --> 00:00:18.818
national security films,
things like that,

00:00:18.818 --> 00:00:21.053
there's this section
in the middle that's,

00:00:21.053 --> 00:00:24.257
you know, this montage of
the reporter like on the phone

00:00:24.257 --> 00:00:27.193
or digging through documents
and things like that,

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:29.562
like entirely unfilmable stuff.

00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:31.331
That's where I live.
I'm the montage.

00:00:31.331 --> 00:00:32.598
-You're just in the montage.
-That's right.

00:00:32.598 --> 00:00:34.133
-No, actually exciting scenes,

00:00:34.133 --> 00:00:35.802
but tiny moments
that have cut together

00:00:35.802 --> 00:00:37.570
to music look interesting.
-Yeah.

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.638
Like, if you were lucky,
you stay in the montage.

00:00:39.638 --> 00:00:41.574
Other times you're like
sweeping on the floor

00:00:41.574 --> 00:00:43.042
and, you know, makeshift bases

00:00:43.042 --> 00:00:45.478
in Iraq and Afghanistan
in the middle of the morning

00:00:45.478 --> 00:00:46.946
waiting for a flight
out somewhere.

00:00:46.946 --> 00:00:50.116
-So this came out.
The timing of this

00:00:50.116 --> 00:00:53.553
was I don't know if you were
what you would call the time--

00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:57.757
but came out two days before
Kabul fell to the Taliban.

00:00:57.757 --> 00:00:59.759
It does seem like
one of the risks here

00:00:59.759 --> 00:01:01.761
is looking at that tiny snapshot

00:01:01.761 --> 00:01:04.196
of the withdrawal
of American troops

00:01:04.196 --> 00:01:09.335
when we have this sort of
20 year war in this country.

00:01:09.335 --> 00:01:13.639
What is your take insofar
as what happened in the days

00:01:13.639 --> 00:01:15.308
after your book was published?

00:01:15.308 --> 00:01:20.146
-So obviously this is a human
disaster, first and foremost.

00:01:20.146 --> 00:01:23.082
And before any of us
get up on our soap boxes,

00:01:23.082 --> 00:01:24.984
that's the most important thing
to recognize.

00:01:24.984 --> 00:01:29.388
This has real impact
on real people's lives.

00:01:29.388 --> 00:01:31.858
What I had a little bit
of trouble

00:01:31.858 --> 00:01:34.127
with watching a lot
of the coverage of it

00:01:34.127 --> 00:01:36.228
is someone who, you know,
not, you know,

00:01:36.228 --> 00:01:37.730
as much as perhaps
some of my colleagues,

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:40.700
but has been to Afghanistan
and reported from there.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:46.339
It was treated as if the end
of this phase of the war

00:01:46.339 --> 00:01:49.575
was divorced from the 20 years
that came before it

00:01:49.575 --> 00:01:53.713
and from being there
and reporting on it throughout.

00:01:53.713 --> 00:01:56.382
It has been very clear
for a very long time

00:01:56.382 --> 00:01:59.986
that the best time to leave
Afghanistan is always yesterday.

00:01:59.986 --> 00:02:02.755
The second best time
is always today

00:02:02.755 --> 00:02:05.024
and the worst time
is always tomorrow.

00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:06.392
And that's what we saw.

00:02:06.392 --> 00:02:10.663
The accumulation of all of
this rot and failure

00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:14.133
and deceit, ultimately,

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:18.771
became what we saw on television
through this searing experience.

00:02:18.771 --> 00:02:22.341
-So there were hearings today,
and we saw senators

00:02:22.341 --> 00:02:25.678
from both sides of the aisle
questioned military leadership.

00:02:25.678 --> 00:02:27.813
And, you know,
because this was 20 years,

00:02:27.813 --> 00:02:30.116
you had a situation
where both parties

00:02:30.116 --> 00:02:34.587
have a great deal of blame
in regards to how this went.

00:02:34.587 --> 00:02:35.955
Did you feel like both parties

00:02:35.955 --> 00:02:38.624
were trying to set
their own narratives

00:02:38.624 --> 00:02:40.726
in their questions today?
What was your takeaway?

00:02:40.726 --> 00:02:42.762
-Yeah, very much so.

00:02:42.762 --> 00:02:44.530
One of the things
that I deal with in the book

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:46.632
is the interplay
between Democrats,

00:02:46.632 --> 00:02:49.235
Republicans
and the security services,

00:02:49.235 --> 00:02:53.907
and we really saw sort of
three phases of delusions

00:02:53.907 --> 00:02:57.043
from each of them from the
Republicans today in testimony.

00:02:57.043 --> 00:03:00.246
We saw this fixation
as has happened

00:03:00.246 --> 00:03:03.916
throughout the War on Terror,
on a conditions-based withdrawal

00:03:03.916 --> 00:03:08.254
and constantly attacking the
guests who were there to testify

00:03:08.254 --> 00:03:10.990
from the military in the
Pentagon about that.

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:12.858
When in fact,
this was never going to be

00:03:12.858 --> 00:03:14.160
a conditions-based withdrawal

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:17.363
because the agreement
that former President Trump

00:03:17.363 --> 00:03:21.967
made with the Taliban stipulated
that as the end of the war.

00:03:21.967 --> 00:03:25.004
Without looking
at that agreement,

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:27.340
you can't really understand
everything that came after.

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:29.742
Similarly, from
the Democratic perspective,

00:03:29.742 --> 00:03:32.745
you had this delusion.
Tim Kaine mentioned it

00:03:32.745 --> 00:03:36.015
that America is turning a page
on the forever war,

00:03:36.015 --> 00:03:39.385
that we're no longer
going to be constantly at war,

00:03:39.385 --> 00:03:41.621
and that is
just simply untrue.

00:03:41.621 --> 00:03:45.424
Not only does the United States
keep 2500 troops in Iraq,

00:03:45.424 --> 00:03:48.260
we just bombed Somalia,
we just bombed Syria.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:52.197
Without rolling back all of the
operations of the War on Terror,

00:03:52.197 --> 00:03:56.068
it is entirely deceitful
to describe the U.S.

00:03:56.068 --> 00:03:59.938
as no longer being at war.
And then from the military side,

00:03:59.938 --> 00:04:01.774
we saw a delusion
that we've seen

00:04:01.774 --> 00:04:03.810
not just throughout
the Afghanistan war,

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:05.711
but throughout the wars
in general,

00:04:05.711 --> 00:04:09.615
which is that the generals
who were testifying talked about

00:04:09.615 --> 00:04:11.784
how last fall
they were recommending.

00:04:11.784 --> 00:04:15.087
And this January, we're
recommending a residual force

00:04:15.087 --> 00:04:17.890
to stay in Afghanistan.
Well, the trouble with that is

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:19.959
there was this agreement
with the Taliban

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:22.762
to keep U.S. forces
in Afghanistan

00:04:22.762 --> 00:04:26.632
would have been violating
the most important aspect

00:04:26.632 --> 00:04:29.402
of that agreement
for a government

00:04:29.402 --> 00:04:33.605
that the United States could
not sustain and in violated --

00:04:33.605 --> 00:04:35.341
in violation
of that agreement,

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:38.677
the Taliban would have made
a withdrawal from Afghanistan,

00:04:38.677 --> 00:04:42.515
even a drawdown of it happening,
one that would occur under fire,

00:04:42.515 --> 00:04:44.917
putting everyone, including
U.S. service members

00:04:44.917 --> 00:04:48.821
and Afghan civilians at far
greater risk, and for what?

00:04:48.821 --> 00:04:51.857
None of them were even inclined
to articulate

00:04:51.857 --> 00:04:54.494
how keeping
a residual force in Afghanistan

00:04:54.494 --> 00:04:58.464
would have done anything other
than keep the U.S. at best,

00:04:58.464 --> 00:05:01.868
perhaps away from the precipice
of the disaster that we've seen.

00:05:01.868 --> 00:05:05.404
And that has been a consistent
feature of the War on Terror

00:05:05.404 --> 00:05:08.274
for most of
the last 20 years.

00:05:08.274 --> 00:05:10.176
-I want to get to former
President Trump,

00:05:10.176 --> 00:05:13.546
but you also talk about
former President Obama's

00:05:13.546 --> 00:05:15.147
missed opportunity

00:05:15.147 --> 00:05:18.351
after the assassination
of Osama bin Laden.

00:05:18.351 --> 00:05:21.120
Where do you feel
like his missteps were?

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:25.992
-So first, it's Obama who really
makes the forever wars forever,

00:05:25.992 --> 00:05:29.595
by entrenching them
in a less conspicuous,

00:05:29.595 --> 00:05:33.065
less ground war
intensive campaign

00:05:33.065 --> 00:05:35.334
that still remains
as global,

00:05:35.334 --> 00:05:37.770
in fact, expands
to more battlefields

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:40.506
than it was under
the Bush administration.

00:05:40.506 --> 00:05:44.744
Without any plausible theory
of how this knits up

00:05:44.744 --> 00:05:47.179
into anything
that can be successful.

00:05:47.179 --> 00:05:49.081
The measurement
of the War on Terror,

00:05:49.081 --> 00:05:52.285
as it became less conspicuous
and more technocratic,

00:05:52.285 --> 00:05:54.854
was that it continue
to be waged,

00:05:54.854 --> 00:05:58.057
not that it be won
or successfully concluded.

00:05:58.057 --> 00:06:03.062
The best time that anyone was
ever going to have to say here

00:06:03.062 --> 00:06:06.432
is a natural point at which
the job the United States

00:06:06.432 --> 00:06:11.070
set out to do after 9-11
is completed is in May 2011

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:14.039
with the assassination
of Osama bin Laden.

00:06:14.039 --> 00:06:18.144
Instead, Obama and his deputy
for counterterrorism,

00:06:18.144 --> 00:06:20.946
John Brennan,
who goes on to be a CIA director

00:06:20.946 --> 00:06:24.717
and presides over a tremendous
amount of the drone war

00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:26.085
that Obama starts,

00:06:26.085 --> 00:06:28.988
is to say that this is
one milestone among many

00:06:28.988 --> 00:06:31.423
and the job of the war
on terror,

00:06:31.423 --> 00:06:34.226
although now it's no longer
called that continues.

00:06:34.226 --> 00:06:37.897
And at that point,
we're unmoored from any real way

00:06:37.897 --> 00:06:40.466
that makes any kind of sense
of concluding this.

00:06:40.466 --> 00:06:45.237
We have been in that position
in the War on Terror longer

00:06:45.237 --> 00:06:49.041
than before the United States
killed Osama bin Laden.

00:06:49.041 --> 00:06:52.311
-You talk about how this
War on Terror,

00:06:52.311 --> 00:06:55.748
the basically American
foreign policy

00:06:55.748 --> 00:06:58.951
after 9/11 sort
of planted the seeds

00:06:58.951 --> 00:07:00.686
that lead to Donald Trump.

00:07:00.686 --> 00:07:04.056
What is what are those seeds
and how do they take root?

00:07:04.056 --> 00:07:08.260
-Yeah. So 9/11
and the U.S. response to it

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:11.731
is this doorway
into the ugliest,

00:07:11.731 --> 00:07:14.767
the most nativist aspects
of American history.

00:07:14.767 --> 00:07:17.803
It's been a lot of talk
about how united America

00:07:17.803 --> 00:07:19.105
was after nine 11.

00:07:19.105 --> 00:07:22.808
In fact, it was marshaled
by political elites,

00:07:22.808 --> 00:07:24.309
primarily in
the Republican Party,

00:07:24.309 --> 00:07:27.679
but with the quick acquiescence
of the Democratic Party

00:07:27.679 --> 00:07:31.350
against a definably
nonwhite enemy

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:37.022
that is both at home and abroad
and the apparatus of repression

00:07:37.022 --> 00:07:39.225
that gets created
and gets expanded,

00:07:39.225 --> 00:07:43.862
all of which takes the form
of loosening restraints,

00:07:43.862 --> 00:07:46.565
legal restraints,
cultural restraints,

00:07:46.565 --> 00:07:50.102
bureaucratic restraints
against surveillance,

00:07:50.102 --> 00:07:54.140
against brutality like torture,
against indefinite detention,

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:58.410
against watch lists
that you can't ever get off.

00:07:58.410 --> 00:08:01.847
All of that becomes entrenched,
institutionalized

00:08:01.847 --> 00:08:06.652
and marshaled against this enemy
that the politics of it have

00:08:06.652 --> 00:08:10.556
as an internal foe
that you're not really --

00:08:10.556 --> 00:08:14.460
if you're, you know, frankly,
a white American able to trust

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:18.664
all of that is marshaled against
our Muslim-American neighbors.

00:08:18.664 --> 00:08:22.535
And once that is unleashed,
once that is normalized,

00:08:22.535 --> 00:08:24.003
once that is sustained,

00:08:24.003 --> 00:08:26.405
once that's shown
that the political system

00:08:26.405 --> 00:08:29.041
will accommodate
that kind of repression

00:08:29.041 --> 00:08:32.612
against this definably
nonwhite internal enemy

00:08:32.612 --> 00:08:34.647
that spreads like wildfire.

00:08:34.647 --> 00:08:36.982
The War on Terror
isn't the only reason

00:08:36.982 --> 00:08:39.018
that Donald Trump
becomes president,

00:08:39.018 --> 00:08:41.620
but the War on Terror
is a pathway to power

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:44.990
under conditions of national
patriotic emergency

00:08:44.990 --> 00:08:49.295
that allows all of that
other racism and nativism

00:08:49.295 --> 00:08:53.465
to unite under
this rubric of emergency.

00:08:53.465 --> 00:08:56.968
-I want to end on something
a little bit more upbeat,

00:08:56.968 --> 00:09:00.705
which is you were going
to be writing a DC comic.

00:09:00.705 --> 00:09:02.308
This is very exciting.

00:09:02.308 --> 00:09:05.043
I love when people
from a world like yours

00:09:05.043 --> 00:09:07.947
take that knowledge
and that experience

00:09:07.947 --> 00:09:09.815
and bring it into
the comic book world.

00:09:09.815 --> 00:09:11.083
And I have to say
it's a pretty good match.

00:09:11.083 --> 00:09:13.052
-What are you writing?
-Well, thanks very much.

00:09:13.052 --> 00:09:16.021
Really pleased to announce it
on your show.

00:09:16.021 --> 00:09:20.091
I'm going to be writing
a Suicide Squad comic for DC

00:09:20.091 --> 00:09:23.395
with my friend Evan Narcisse,
who wrote an amazing book

00:09:23.395 --> 00:09:25.998
for Marvel,
"Rise of the Black Panther."

00:09:25.998 --> 00:09:31.236
We're going to be taking
on a character, Amanda Waller,

00:09:31.236 --> 00:09:33.873
who viewers of this show
may know

00:09:33.873 --> 00:09:36.742
as the Viola Davis character
in the movies.

00:09:36.742 --> 00:09:40.813
She is the big intelligence
figure in the DC Universe,

00:09:40.813 --> 00:09:42.714
a character I absolutely love.

00:09:42.714 --> 00:09:44.783
And next year,
if you're wondering,

00:09:44.783 --> 00:09:47.620
you know, how does
this guy's critique work,

00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:50.589
perhaps in a superhero universe?
-Right.

00:09:50.589 --> 00:09:52.024
-We'll be answering
that question for you.

00:09:52.024 --> 00:09:54.292
-Alright. But I do think people
should read this one first

00:09:54.292 --> 00:09:56.895
and then enjoy
the comic book as a treat.

00:09:56.895 --> 00:09:58.163
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:09:58.163 --> 00:10:00.165
It's a wonderful book,
and I really appreciate

00:10:00.165 --> 00:10:02.401
you spending time with us,
"Reign of Terror" is out now.

